[{"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 0, "text": "Chapter 1 : INTRODUCTION Project is about emotional AI companion designed to provide supportive, empathetic, and human-like interaction. Unlike traditional chatbots that focus only on information delivery, Lifa understands user emotions through sentiment analysis and adapts its responses accordingly. The system integrates text-based conversation with voice output, creating a more natural and comforting communication experience. Built using Streamlit and powered by Google Gemini AI, Lifa offers f"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 1, "text": "rting communication experience. Built using Streamlit and powered by Google Gemini AI, Lifa offers features such as mood tracking, personalized responses, and real-time emotional tone adjustment. The primary aim of this project is to enhance human-computer interaction by creating an AI that listens, cares, and responds with emotional awareness. 1.1 Problem Definition Most existing chatbots are task-oriented and lack emotional understanding, which makes interactions feel mechanical and impersonal"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 2, "text": "k-oriented and lack emotional understanding, which makes interactions feel mechanical and impersonal. These systems are primarily designed to answer queries, provide information, or perform specific actions without considering the emotional state of the user. As a result, users who seek emotional support, reassurance, or companionship often feel disconnected when interacting with such systems, especially during moments of stress, loneliness, anxiety, or sadness. In real-life human communication,"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 3, "text": "ecially during moments of stress, loneliness, anxiety, or sadness. In real-life human communication, emotions play a crucial role in building trust and meaningful connections. The absence of emotional intelligence in conventional AI systems limits their effectiveness in scenarios that require empathy, compassion, and emotional sensitivity. This creates a gap between human expectations and machine capabilities. Therefore, there is a growing need for an AI system that can not only process language"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 4, "text": "capabilities. Therefore, there is a growing need for an AI system that can not only process language but also understand the emotional context behind user messages. Such a system should be capable of analyzing user sentiment, adapting its tone, and responding in a warm, supportive, and human-like manner. The goal is to move beyond robotic responses and develop an AI companion that provides emotional comfort, improves user well-being, and enhances the overall quality of human-computer interaction"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 5, "text": "al comfort, improves user well-being, and enhances the overall quality of human-computer interaction. 1.2. Project Overview The project \"Lifa \u2013 An Emotional AI Companion\" focuses on developing an intelligent chatbot that is capable of understanding and responding to human emotions. The system is designed to go beyond traditional question-answer mechanisms by providing emotionally supportive and empathetic responses. It uses sentiment analysis to detect the emotional tone of user messages and adj"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 6, "text": "pathetic responses. It uses sentiment analysis to detect the emotional tone of user messages and adjusts its response style accordingly, making the interaction feel more natural and personalized. Lifa is implemented as a web-based application using the Streamlit framework, allowing users to interact with it through a simple and user-friendly interface. The application integrates Google Gemini AI for generating intelligent responses and gTTS (Google Text-to-Speech) for converting text responses i"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 7, "text": "or generating intelligent responses and gTTS (Google Text-to-Speech) for converting text responses into voice, enabling dual-mode communication (text + audio). The major objectives of the project are: To build an AI system that understands emotional context To provide empathetic and supportive responses To enhance user experience through voice interaction To track and analyze the user\u2019s emotional trends Overall, the project aims to create a virtual emotional companion that offers comfort, compan"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 8, "text": "trends Overall, the project aims to create a virtual emotional companion that offers comfort, companionship, and meaningful interaction, helping users feel heard and supported. 1.3 Hardware Specification The hardware requirements for the Lifa AI Companion system are minimal, making it accessible and easy to deploy on common computing devices. The system does not require specialized hardware and can run efficiently on standard personal computers or laptops. The recommended hardware configuration "}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 9, "text": "n run efficiently on standard personal computers or laptops. The recommended hardware configuration is as follows: Processor: Intel i3 / AMD Ryzen 3 or higher RAM: Minimum 4GB (8GB recommended for smoother performance) Storage: At least 500MB of free disk space Internet Connection: Stable broadband connection for real-time API communication Audio Output Device: Speakers or headphones for voice playback Display: Standard monitor with minimum resolution of 1366x768 These specifications ensure smoo"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 10, "text": "yback Display: Standard monitor with minimum resolution of 1366x768 These specifications ensure smooth operation of the application, including real-time sentiment analysis, AI response generation, and voice output. 1.3 Hardware Specification The Lifa AI Companion is developed using Python 3.x and the Streamlit framework to provide a user-friendly web interface. It uses Google Gemini Generative AI for intelligent response generation and gTTS for converting text into speech. Supporting libraries s"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 11, "text": "for intelligent response generation and gTTS for converting text into speech. Supporting libraries such as streamlit, google.generativeai, socket, io, and base64 enable smooth functionality and communication. The system runs on Windows, Linux, or macOS and can be accessed through modern web browsers like Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, and Microsoft Edge. These tools work together to ensure efficient emotional analysis and interactive user experience. The Lifa AI Companion system is developed us"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 12, "text": "ent emotional analysis and interactive user experience. The Lifa AI Companion system is developed using modern software tools and technologies that support artificial intelligence, web interaction, and voice processing. The software environment is designed to ensure stability, scalability, and ease of use. 1.4 Scope of the Project The scope of the Lifa AI Companion project defines the boundaries and capabilities of the system. This project focuses on developing an emotionally intelligent chatbot"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 13, "text": "nd capabilities of the system. This project focuses on developing an emotionally intelligent chatbot that can interact with users through text and voice while providing empathetic and supportive responses. The scope of the system includes: Real-time text-based chat interaction Sentiment analysis of user messages Emotion-aware response generation Voice output with adjustable gender and speed Mood tracking and emotional trend visualization Secure API integration with Google Gemini Simple and user-"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 14, "text": "racking and emotional trend visualization Secure API integration with Google Gemini Simple and user-friendly web interface The project does not include advanced features such as facial emotion recognition, speech-to-text input, long-term memory storage, or psychological diagnosis. These can be considered for future enhancement. Overall, the scope is limited to creating a supportive virtual companion that improves emotional communication and enhances user experience through intelligent and empath"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 15, "text": "on that improves emotional communication and enhances user experience through intelligent and empathetic interaction. Chapter 2: LITERATURE SURVEY This chapter reviews existing research and technologies related to chatbot systems, emotional AI, and intelligent human-computer interaction. It highlights the limitations of current systems and justifies the need for an emotionally intelligent companion like Lifa. 2.1 Existing System Existing systems mainly consist of traditional chatbots and AI assi"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 16, "text": "n like Lifa. 2.1 Existing System Existing systems mainly consist of traditional chatbots and AI assistants that focus on providing predefined or informational responses. These systems are efficient in handling basic queries but lack emotional intelligence, personalized interaction, and real-time sentiment-based response adaptation. As a result, they often feel mechanical and are not suitable for users seeking emotional support or human-like companionship. 2.2 Proposed System 2.2.1 Overview of Li"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 17, "text": "sers seeking emotional support or human-like companionship. 2.2 Proposed System 2.2.1 Overview of Lifa Lifa is an emotionally intelligent AI companion designed to understand user emotions and respond in a caring and supportive manner, creating a more human-like conversation. 2.2.2 Key Features Sentiment-driven emotional response generation Real-time emotion detection Voice-enabled responses Personalized replies Mood tracking dashboard 2.2.3 Advanced Capabilities Dynamic personality adaptation Em"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 18, "text": "alized replies Mood tracking dashboard 2.2.3 Advanced Capabilities Dynamic personality adaptation Emotion-based tone modulation Secure API integration Multi-modal interaction (text + voice) 2.2.4 Benefits of Proposed System Enhanced emotional engagement Improved user satisfaction Natural and empathetic communication Supportive digital companionship 2.3 Feasibility Study The feasibility study evaluates whether the Lifa AI Companion system can be successfully implemented in real-world conditions. "}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 19, "text": "ates whether the Lifa AI Companion system can be successfully implemented in real-world conditions. Technically, the project is feasible due to the availability of powerful tools like Google Gemini AI for text generation, Streamlit for user interface development, and gTTS for voice synthesis. These technologies are reliable, well-documented, and widely used, ensuring smooth integration and stable performance. The use of cloud-based AI models also allows the system to process user input efficient"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 20, "text": "performance. The use of cloud-based AI models also allows the system to process user input efficiently and deliver accurate, emotionally adaptive responses in real time. Operationally, the system is easy to use, requires minimal training, and can be deployed on standard computers with basic hardware requirements. The user-friendly Streamlit interface ensures accessibility for users of all technical backgrounds, while the automated processes such as sentiment detection and response generation red"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 21, "text": "l backgrounds, while the automated processes such as sentiment detection and response generation reduce manual complexity. This makes the system suitable for continuous usage in personal or institutional environments. Economically, the project is cost-effective as it relies on open-source technologies and does not require expensive infrastructure or specialized hardware. Maintenance costs are also low, and future upgrades can be implemented without significant financial burden. Additionally, the"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 22, "text": " low, and future upgrades can be implemented without significant financial burden. Additionally, the scalability of the system allows it to grow in functionality without major cost increases. Overall, the Lifa AI Companion system is practical, efficient, scalable, and suitable for real-time emotional interaction applications. Its feasibility across technical, operational, and economic dimensions makes it a strong candidate for real-world deployment in emotional support and interactive AI platfor"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 23, "text": "akes it a strong candidate for real-world deployment in emotional support and interactive AI platforms. 2.3 SYSTEM ANALYSIS & DESIGN The third objective is to enable the system to speak back to the user through text-to-speech (TTS) synthesis, completing the conversational loop. This is accomplished using Python\u2019s pyttsx3 library, which converts textual responses into audible output. The pyttsx3 engine provides control over voice rate, volume, and tone, allowing a natural and human-like speech ex"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 24, "text": "gine provides control over voice rate, volume, and tone, allowing a natural and human-like speech experience. Unlike online TTS services, pyttsx3 operates offline, ensuring reliability and quick responses without needing an internet connection. The speak() function initializes the TTS engine, queues the text, and plays it through the speaker. Each response is clear, synchronized, and directly related to the recognized command. For example, if the user says \u201chello,\u201d the assistant replies with \u201cHe"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 25, "text": "ted to the recognized command. For example, if the user says \u201chello,\u201d the assistant replies with \u201cHello, how can I help you?\u201d This direct auditory feedback enhances interactivity and engagement. This objective demonstrates how artificial intelligence systems can express themselves audibly, providing immediate feedback. It transforms the system from a silent interpreter into an interactive companion capable of real-time communication. The speak() function initializes the TTS engine, queues the te"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 26, "text": "n capable of real-time communication. The speak() function initializes the TTS engine, queues the text, and plays it through the speaker. Each response is clear, synchronized, and directly related to the recognized command. In conclusion, this objective ensures that the assistant not only understands but also communicates effectively, establishing a two-way interaction\u2014a hallmark of intelligent systems. 2.4 Research Gap 2.4.1 Lack of Emotional Intelligence in Existing Systems Most existing chatb"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 27, "text": "stems. 2.4 Research Gap 2.4.1 Lack of Emotional Intelligence in Existing Systems Most existing chatbot systems focus on task completion and information delivery, but fail to understand and respond appropriately to human emotions. This limits their effectiveness in providing emotional support. 2.4.2 Absence of Real-Time Sentiment Adaptation Current AI systems do not dynamically adjust their response tone based on the user's emotional state, resulting in generic and emotionally insensitive replies"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 28, "text": "e tone based on the user's emotional state, resulting in generic and emotionally insensitive replies. 2.4.3 Limited Focus on User Well-being Many chatbots are designed for business or technical purposes rather than user emotional well-being, creating a gap in emotionally supportive AI applications. 2.4.4 Need for Voice-Enabled Emotional Interaction Existing systems rarely combine emotional understanding with voice output, reducing the sense of companionship and natural interaction. 2.5 To demons"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 29, "text": "anding with voice output, reducing the sense of companionship and natural interaction. 2.5 To demonstrate basic human\u2013AI interaction principles The Lifa AI Companion can be used in mental health support platforms, virtual therapy systems, educational guidance tools, customer care services, and personal wellness applications. This objective demonstrates how artificial intelligence systems can express themselves audibly, providing immediate feedback. It transforms the system from a silent interpre"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 30, "text": "ss themselves audibly, providing immediate feedback. It transforms the system from a silent interpreter into an interactive companion capable of real-time communication. The speak() function initializes the TTS engine, queues the text, and plays it through the speaker. It can help users manage stress, provide emotional companionship, and improve overall user engagement through empathetic interaction. Chapter 3: SYSTEM ANALYSIS & DESIGN This chapter explains the detailed analysis and design of th"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 31, "text": "on. Chapter 3: SYSTEM ANALYSIS & DESIGN This chapter explains the detailed analysis and design of the Lifa AI Companion system. It describes the system requirements, architecture, data flow, and design methodology used to build the application. The aim of this chapter is to provide a clear technical understanding of how the system operates internally and how different modules work together to produce an emotionally intelligent response. This chapter also defines the functional and non-functional"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 32, "text": "uce an emotionally intelligent response. This chapter also defines the functional and non-functional requirements of the system and explains how these requirements are converted into a structured and efficient system design. It highlights the interaction between the user interface, sentiment analysis module, AI response generator, and voice output module. The workflow ensures that the system responds accurately, empathetically, and in real time. Overall, this chapter acts as the technical backbo"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 33, "text": "nds accurately, empathetically, and in real time. Overall, this chapter acts as the technical backbone of the project, explaining the logical structure and operational flow of the Lifa AI Companion. 3.1 Requirement Specification 3.1.1 Functional Requirements The functional requirements define what the system must do: The system should allow users to enter text messages. The system should analyze the emotional sentiment of user input. The system should generate emotionally appropriate responses. "}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 34, "text": "he emotional sentiment of user input. The system should generate emotionally appropriate responses. The system should provide voice output for responses. The system should display sentiment labels for each message. The system should maintain mood history for tracking. The system should allow secure API key input. 3.1.2 Non-Functional Requirements These specify how the system should perform: The system should be user-friendly and easy to operate. Response time should be fast and efficient. The sy"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 35, "text": "stem should be user-friendly and easy to operate. Response time should be fast and efficient. The system should ensure reliability and stability. The interface should be visually simple and attractive. The system should securely handle user data and API keys. The system should work smoothly on standard hardware. 3.2 System Architecture The Lifa AI Companion follows a modular architecture where each component performs a specific function. The front-end interface is developed using Streamlit, whic"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 36, "text": "h component performs a specific function. The front-end interface is developed using Streamlit, which allows users to interact with the system through a web-based platform. The backend uses Google Gemini AI to generate intelligent responses and gTTS to convert text responses into speech. Major Components: User Interface (Streamlit Web Application) Sentiment Analysis Module AI Response Generator (Google Gemini API) Voice Output Module (gTTS) Mood Tracking Module Internet Connectivity Checker This"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 37, "text": "oogle Gemini API) Voice Output Module (gTTS) Mood Tracking Module Internet Connectivity Checker This architecture ensures efficient data flow and smooth interaction between the modules to deliver a seamless user experience. he workflow ensures that the system responds accurately, empathetically, and in real time. Overall, this chapter acts as the technical backbone of the project, explaining the logical structure and operational flow of the Lifa AI Companion. Chapter 4 : Conclusion and Future Sc"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 38, "text": "ogical structure and operational flow of the Lifa AI Companion. Chapter 4 : Conclusion and Future Scope 4.1 Conclusion The Voice-Controlled Assistant successfully demonstrates the integration of speech recognition, text-to-speech synthesis, and graphical user interface elements to create a functional, real-time, interactive system. The assistant effectively captures live voice input, converts it into text, and responds audibly while simultaneously displaying the transcript and audio waveform. Th"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 39, "text": "nto text, and responds audibly while simultaneously displaying the transcript and audio waveform. The combination of SpeechRecognition, pyttsx3, and Tkinter libraries ensures smooth, responsive operation with minimal latency. The project highlights key principles of human\u2013AI interaction, such as natural communication, accessibility, and real-time feedback. Users can operate the system hands-free, making it suitable for educational purposes, accessibility applications for differently-abled indivi"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 40, "text": "making it suitable for educational purposes, accessibility applications for differently-abled individuals, and as a prototype for advanced voice-based assistants. The modular design also allows for easy maintenance and future enhancements, demonstrating the potential of open-source tools in AI-driven applications. 4.2 Future Enhancements While the system performs reliably, several improvements can be implemented to extend its capabilities. Future enhancements may include: Integration with Natura"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 41, "text": " be implemented to extend its capabilities. Future enhancements may include: Integration with Natural Language Processing (NLP): Enabling contextual understanding and intelligent responses. Wake-word detection: Allowing the system to activate with phrases like \u201cHey Assistant\u201d for improved usability. Multi-language support: Expanding recognition and response to multiple languages. Network-based voice control: Using sockets for remote or IoT applications. Advanced GUI features: Adding conversation"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 42, "text": "ce control: Using sockets for remote or IoT applications. Advanced GUI features: Adding conversation logs, voice customization, and interactive visualizations to improve user experience. These improvements would transform the basic prototype into a more versatile and intelligent voice assistant capable of functioning in real-world applications. The current system lays a solid foundation for such future development. Beyond functional demonstration, this project highlights the potential of open-so"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 43, "text": "uture development. Beyond functional demonstration, this project highlights the potential of open-source tools in building intelligent systems. Libraries such as SpeechRecognition and pyttsx3 provide a practical and cost-effective way to develop sophisticated AI-based applications without complex hardware or cloud-based dependencies. The modular design of the system allows for scalability, maintainability, and ease of future enhancements, including advanced natural language understanding, wake-w"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 44, "text": "nability, and ease of future enhancements, including advanced natural language understanding, wake-word activation, and multi-language support. In conclusion, the Voice-Controlled Assistant serves as a robust prototype for real-time voice interaction, laying a solid foundation for further research and development in AI-driven conversational systems. It successfully combines technical innovation, user-centered design, and accessibility principles, demonstrating the transformative potential of voi"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "themeptoject_V.docx", "chunk_index": 45, "text": "ser-centered design, and accessibility principles, demonstrating the transformative potential of voice-based human\u2013computer interaction in modern computing environments."}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "text": "Social Network Analysis (SNA) Simple Detailed Notes 1. Degree Centrality Degree centrality means the number of direct connections a node has in a network. Formula: CD(v) = deg(v) Example: If node A is connected to B, C and D then: Degree(A) = 3 Uses: (cid:127) Finding active users in social media (cid:127) Detecting popular people in a network Advantages: (cid:127) Easy to calculate (cid:127) Simple to understand Disadvantages: (cid:127) Only considers direct neighbours 2. Closeness Centrality C"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "text": "le to understand Disadvantages: (cid:127) Only considers direct neighbours 2. Closeness Centrality Closeness centrality measures how close a node is to all other nodes. Formula: CC(v) = (n-1) / Sd(v,u) Where: (cid:127) n = total nodes (cid:127) d(v,u) = shortest distance Example: Distances from A: AfiB = 1 AfiC = 2 AfiD = 1 Total distance = 4 CC(A) = 3/4 = 0.75 Uses: (cid:127) Fast information spreading (cid:127) Communication systems 3. Betweenness Centrality Betweenness centrality measures how"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "text": "eading (cid:127) Communication systems 3. Betweenness Centrality Betweenness centrality measures how often a node lies between shortest paths. Formula: CB(v) = S[sst(v)/sst] Example: If node B lies on 3 shortest paths out of 5: CB(B) = 3/5 = 0.6 Uses: (cid:127) Finding bridge nodes (cid:127) Traffic routing (cid:127) Criminal network analysis 4. Eigenvector Centrality Eigenvector centrality measures node importance based on important neighbours. Formula: Ax = lx Meaning: A node connected to impo"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "text": "es node importance based on important neighbours. Formula: Ax = lx Meaning: A node connected to important nodes becomes important. Uses: (cid:127) Google PageRank (cid:127) Recommendation systems 5. Adjacency Matrix Adjacency matrix is a square matrix representation of a graph. If nodes are connected fi 1 If nodes are not connected fi 0 Example: A B C A 0 1 1 B 1 0 0 C 1 0 0 Advantages: (cid:127) Easy edge checking Disadvantages: (cid:127) Uses more memory 6. Adjacency List Adjacency list stores"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "text": "Easy edge checking Disadvantages: (cid:127) Uses more memory 6. Adjacency List Adjacency list stores neighbours of each node. Example: A fi B,C B fi A C fi A Advantages: (cid:127) Memory efficient (cid:127) Best for sparse graphs 7. Degree of Node Degree means total number of edges connected to a node. Formula: deg(v) = number of connected edges Example: If node X is connected to 5 nodes: Degree(X) = 5 8. Path A path is a sequence of connected nodes. Example: A fi B fi C fi D Path length = 3 Typ"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "text": ") = 5 8. Path A path is a sequence of connected nodes. Example: A fi B fi C fi D Path length = 3 Types: (cid:127) Simple path (cid:127) Shortest path (cid:127) Closed path 9. Diameter Diameter is the longest shortest path between any two nodes. Formula: Diameter = max(d(u,v)) Example: Shortest paths: AfiB = 1 AfiC = 2 AfiD = 3 Diameter = 3 Uses: (cid:127) Measures network size 10. Macroscopic Analysis Macroscopic analysis studies the whole network. Focus: (cid:127) Density (cid:127) Diameter (ci"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "text": "ysis Macroscopic analysis studies the whole network. Focus: (cid:127) Density (cid:127) Diameter (cid:127) Average path length Example: Complete Facebook network 11. Mesoscopic Analysis Mesoscopic analysis studies groups or communities. Focus: (cid:127) Clusters (cid:127) Communities Example: Friend groups in class 12. Microscopic Analysis Microscopic analysis studies individual nodes. Focus: (cid:127) Centrality (cid:127) Influence Example: Most influential student 13. Unipartite Graph Uniparti"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "text": ":127) Centrality (cid:127) Influence Example: Most influential student 13. Unipartite Graph Unipartite graph contains only one type of node. Example: Student friendship network Characteristics: (cid:127) Same type of nodes 14. Bipartite Graph Bipartite graph contains two different sets of nodes. Example: Students and Courses Applications: (cid:127) Recommendation systems 15. Directed Graph Directed graph contains edges with direction. Example: A fi B Instagram follow system is directed because r"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "text": "d graph contains edges with direction. Example: A fi B Instagram follow system is directed because relation is not always mutual. 16. Undirected Graph Undirected graph contains edges without direction. Example: A \u2014 B Facebook friendship is undirected because friendship is mutual. 17. K-Clique A clique is a fully connected group. Example: 3 friends connected with each other. Properties: (cid:127) Strong community (cid:127) Dense graph 18. K-Plex K-plex is a relaxed form of clique. In a k-plex of "}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "text": "trong community (cid:127) Dense graph 18. K-Plex K-plex is a relaxed form of clique. In a k-plex of size n: Every node connects to at least n-k nodes. Advantages: (cid:127) Flexible (cid:127) Realistic social structure 19. Community Detection Community detection means finding groups with strong internal connections. Methods: (cid:127) Girvan-Newman (cid:127) Louvain Method (cid:127) Label Propagation Applications: (cid:127) Fraud detection (cid:127) Social media analysis 20. Common Neighbour Sco"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 10, "text": "ion Applications: (cid:127) Fraud detection (cid:127) Social media analysis 20. Common Neighbour Score Counts common neighbours between two nodes. Formula: CN(x,y) = |G(x) \u02d9 G(y)| Example: A = {B,C,D} F = {C,D,E} Common neighbours = {C,D} CN(A,F) = 2 21. Jaccard Similarity Measures similarity between neighbour sets. Formula: J(x,y) = Intersection / Union Example: Intersection = 2 Union = 4 J(A,F) = 2/4 = 0.5 Range: 0 to 1 22. Adamic-Adar Score Gives more importance to rare neighbours. Formula: A"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 11, "text": "= 2/4 = 0.5 Range: 0 to 1 22. Adamic-Adar Score Gives more importance to rare neighbours. Formula: AA(x,y) = S[1/log(deg(z))] Example: deg(C)=2 deg(D)=3 AA = 1/log2 + 1/log3 Applications: (cid:127) Friend recommendation 23. Cascading Cascade means spreading of information or behaviour. Examples: (cid:127) Viral videos (cid:127) Rumours (cid:127) Disease spread 24. Full Cascade In full cascade all nodes adopt the behaviour. Example: Everyone installs an application. 25. Partial Cascade In partial"}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 12, "text": "nodes adopt the behaviour. Example: Everyone installs an application. 25. Partial Cascade In partial cascade only some nodes adopt behaviour. Example: Only some users share a video. 26. Threshold Model A node becomes active if enough neighbours are active. Formula: m/k \u2021 f Where: (cid:127) m = active neighbours (cid:127) k = total neighbours (cid:127) f = threshold Example: Total neighbours = 4 Active neighbours = 3 3/4 = 0.75 If threshold = 0.5 then node becomes active."}, {"document_id": 2, "filename": "SNA_Simple_Separate_Notes.pdf", "chunk_index": 13, "text": "tive neighbours = 3 3/4 = 0.75 If threshold = 0.5 then node becomes active."}]